{
  "teamId": 111,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Boston Red Sox were a team that confounded expectations from the very first week. Written off by many before the season began, they burst out of the gate in April with a 17-10 record, fueled by explosive offense and timely clutch hitting. J.D. Martinez's two-run double on April 6th alone shifted win probability by more than three-quarters, a sign of things to come from an offense that would ultimately score 829 runs on the year. The Red Sox weren't supposed to be this good — and they seemed determined to prove everyone wrong.\n\nMay and June carried the momentum forward, with Boston posting a combined 33-21 record over those two months. The power was real: Rafael Devers was mashing, Alex Verdugo was coming up big, and Christian Arroyo's grand slam in mid-June punctuated a stretch of dominant baseball. The shadow hanging over this era, however, was closer Matt Barnes. Across several high-leverage appearances, Barnes surrendered crushing home runs to the likes of Shohei Ohtani and Vladimir Guerrero Jr., a recurring vulnerability that the team managed to paper over with its potent lineup but that hinted at trouble ahead.\n\nAugust was where the wheels wobbled. The team went 12-16, their only losing month of the season, and Barnes continued to hemorrhage leads at the worst moments. George Springer's three-run blast on August 8th cost nearly 0.7 win probability in a single swing. Yet even in the gloom, the offense kept fighting — Devers doubled home runs, Kyle Schwarber launched bombs after arriving at the trade deadline, and the team refused to fully collapse. September brought a steadying 14-11 record, capped by a late-season sweep that clinched a postseason berth.\n\nOctober told a story of thrilling ascent and heartbreaking ceiling. The Red Sox dispatched the Tampa Bay Rays in a dramatic ALDS, with Christian Vázquez and Rafael Devers delivering clutch postseason power. Then came Houston. The Red Sox gave the Astros everything they had — J.D. Martinez crushed a grand slam in Game 2 to keep Boston alive — but Nathan Eovaldi's late-inning surrender of the go-ahead run in Game 6 ended the dream. A 92-win season, a division rival vanquished, and a return to October glory: it wasn't a championship, but for a team nobody believed in, it was a season worth celebrating.",
  "chapters": [
